Time Complexity of Recursive Factorial Program:

Case            	Complexity	
Best Case	        O(1)	
Average Case    	O(n)	
Worst Case	        O(n)	
Space Complexity:   O(n)

Conclusion:

The Recursive Factorial Program calculates the factorial of a given number using recursion. The function repeatedly calls itself with n - 1 until it reaches the base case, where n is either 0 or 1.
The time complexity is O(n) in the average and worst cases because the function makes approximately n recursive calls. The space complexity is O(n) because all recursive function calls are stored in the recursion call stack.
Although recursion makes the program simple and easy to understand, it requires more memory than the iterative factorial approach. The iterative approach uses O(1) auxiliary space, whereas the recursive approach requires O(n) auxiliary space.
